=F0=9F=92=A1 How to spot high-demand dates early

=F0=9F=93=88 Watch three signals, not just one

High-demand dates rarely announce themselves. Look for:

- Booking pace: Are you picking up faster than at this point last year? If =
a date had two reservations then and has six now, something is driving dema=
nd.
- Local events: Concerts, festivals, trade fairs and school breaks. Check y=
our event calendar every week.
- Competitor behavior: Rising rates or sell-outs nearby are a prompt to loo=
k closer at your own dates.

=F0=9F=97=93=EF=B8=8F Make it a weekly habit (under an hour)

- Monday (15 to 20 min): Scan the next 30 to 60 days of reservations. Compa=
re pickup to last year and check events for the same dates.
- Mid-week (5 to 10 min): Check competitor rates on the OTAs for the dates =
you flagged.
- End of week (10 min): Decide on a rate move before the weekend. Even a sm=
all step up beats waiting.

=F0=9F=AA=9C Raise rates in steps

Test the market with a small increase first. A sudden spike can push early =
bookers to competitors. When your best nights start filling up, a two-night=
 minimum on Friday and Saturday can help you avoid losing Sunday. Keep your=
 bigger rate moves for dates where bookings are strong, there=E2=80=99s a c=
lear local reason for demand, and competitors are filling up too.

=F0=9F=8E=AF Use competitor rates as a prompt, not a rule

A competitor selling out at =E2=82=AC150 shows demand is there. It doesn=E2=
=80=99t set your price, because their occupancy, costs and room mix differ =
from yours. If your pickup is strong, your ceiling may be higher. After eac=
h busy period, compare actual occupancy, ADR and RevPAR with what you expec=
ted.

