eight: inherit;" data-hs-cos-general-type=3D"widget" data-hs-cos-type=3D"ri=
ch_text"><h2 style=3D"margin:0; line-height:175%; font-size:18px; color:#00=
686e">=F0=9F=93=88 Watch three signals, not just one</h2>
<p style=3D"line-height:175%">High-demand dates rarely announce themselves.=
 Look for:</p>
<ul style=3D"line-height:175%">
<li><span style=3D"color: #fb6214;"><strong>Booking pace:</strong></span> A=
re you picking up faster than at this point last year? If a date had two re=
servations then and has six now, something is driving demand.</li>
<li><span style=3D"color: #fb6214;"><strong>Local events:</strong> </span>C=
oncerts, festivals, trade fairs and school breaks. Check your event calenda=
r every week.</li>
<li><span style=3D"color: #fb6214;"><strong>Competitor behavior:</strong></=
span> Rising rates or sell-outs nearby are a prompt to look closer at your =
own dates.</li>
</ul>
<h2 style=3D"margin:0; line-height:175%; font-size:18px; color:#00686e">=F0=
=9F=97=93=EF=B8=8F Make it a weekly habit (under an hour)</h2>
<ul style=3D"line-height:175%">
<li><span style=3D"color: #fb6214;"><strong>Monday (15 to 20 min):</strong>=
</span> Scan the next 30 to 60 days of reservations. Compare pickup to last=
 year and check events for the same dates.</li>
<li><span style=3D"color: #fb6214;"><strong>Mid-week (5 to 10 min):</strong=
></span> Check competitor rates on the OTAs for the dates you flagged.</li>
<li><span style=3D"color: #fb6214;"><strong>End of week (10 min):</strong> =
</span>Decide on a rate move before the weekend. Even a small step up beats=
 waiting.</li>
</ul>
<ul style=3D"line-height:175%"></ul>
<h2 style=3D"margin:0; line-height:175%; font-size:18px; color:#00686e">=F0=
=9F=AA=9C Raise rates in steps</h2>
<p style=3D"line-height:175%">Test the market with a <span style=3D"color: =
#fb6214;"><strong>small increase first</strong>.</span> A sudden spike can =
push early bookers to competitors. When your best nights start filling up, =
a two-night minimum on Friday and Saturday can help you avoid losing Sunday=
. Keep your bigger rate moves for dates where bookings are strong, there=E2=
=80=99s a clear local reason for demand, and competitors are filling up too=
.</p>
<div style=3D"line-height:175%; width:100%">
<div style=3D"line-height:175%; height:0px" height=3D"0">&nbsp;</div>
</div>
<ul style=3D"line-height:175%"></ul>
<h2 style=3D"margin:0; line-height:175%; font-size:18px; color:#00686e">=F0=
=9F=8E=AF Use competitor rates as a prompt, not a rule</h2>
<ul style=3D"line-height:175%"></ul>
<p style=3D"line-height:175%">A competitor selling out at =E2=82=AC150 show=
s demand is there. It doesn=E2=80=99t set <span style=3D"color: #fb6214;"><=
strong>your</strong> </span>price, because their occupancy, costs and room =
mix differ from yours. If your pickup is strong, your ceiling may be higher=
. After each busy period, compare actual occupancy, ADR and RevPAR with wha=
t you expected.</p>
<ul style=3D"line-height:175%"></ul>
<ul style=3D"line-height:175%"></ul></div></div></td></tr></tbody></table>
